(3520224, 489306, 10402, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by photochild405:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by noisemaker:</b></div><hr/><i> this reminds me of <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=68346" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">dainmcgowan</a> shot<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=482153" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/646/thumb/482153.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/646/thumb/482153.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/646/thumb/482153.jpg"/></a> </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Woaaa I''m confused that looks like the same exact shot </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Like Chris said in his description, it''s a rather clichéd shot. A lot of people take similar pictures of this landmark. It just goes to show that even clichés can win ribbons if very well done, as this certainly is! It''s a great example of how useful Graduated ND filters can be. Most London Eye At Night photos have a much darker sky with overexposed foreground lights. The exposure here is perfect. I also like the unlit trees, which don''t detract from the subject like the typical photos do.</td>', 'Originally posted by photochild405: Originally posted by noisemaker: this reminds me of  dainmcgowan shot    Woaaa I''m confused that looks like the same exact shot   Like Chris said in his description, it''s a rather clichéd shot. A lot of people take similar pictures of this landmark. It just goes to show that even clichés can win ribbons if very well done, as this certainly is! It''s a great example of how useful Graduated ND filters can be. Most London Eye At Night photos have a much darker sky with overexposed foreground lights. The exposure here is perfect. I also like the unlit trees, which don''t detract from the subject like the typical photos do.', '2007-04-04 20:29:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(3520408, 489429, 76094, '<td valign="top">That is amazing!</td>', 'That is amazing!', '2007-04-04 21:41:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(3520497, 489496, 15711, '<td valign="top">Great job, Shannon.  Congrats on 4th</td>', 'Great job, Shannon.  Congrats on 4th', '2007-04-04 22:12:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(3520681, 489429, 47541, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Fit: This fits the challenge well.  We have the flames clearly in a spinning pattern.  No DNMC''s for this one, and that shows in that you got not votes below a 4 and you are in the top 10.<br/> <br/>Composition: The person waving the fire around is nicely positioned in the bottom right with that tree filling the middle to top right.  This gives us some nice direction for our eye to travel form the fire, to the tree, and then settle back on the fire.  Is the area with the castle needed?  I think it is.<br/> <br/>Technical: You have captured the flames nicely.  There is a nice warm feel to the image.<br/> <br/>Feel: I like this image but I feel that it''s a bit too orange.  The red/orange of the flames is just a little "hidden" by the orange fog.  I gave it an 8 so clearly I liked it.  Great work.<br/> <br/>PM me if you have any questions</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Fit: This fits the challenge well.  We have the flames clearly in a spinning pattern.  No DNMC''s for this one, and that shows in that you got not votes below a 4 and you are in the top 10. Composition: The person waving the fire around is nicely positioned in the bottom right with that tree filling the middle to top right.  This gives us some nice direction for our eye to travel form the fire, to the tree, and then settle back on the fire.  Is the area with the castle needed?  I think it is. Technical: You have captured the flames nicely.  There is a nice warm feel to the image. Feel: I like this image but I feel that it''s a bit too orange.  The red/orange of the flames is just a little "hidden" by the orange fog.  I gave it an 8 so clearly I liked it.  Great work. PM me if you have any questions', '2007-04-04 22:58:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(3520709, 489574, 72317, '<td valign="top">It has to do with spinning because owls rotate their heads. It appears the owl is rotating his head in the picture, too. Nobody else thought of it.</td>', 'It has to do with spinning because owls rotate their heads. It appears the owl is rotating his head in the picture, too. Nobody else thought of it.', '2007-04-04 23:03:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(3520728, 489281, 72317, '<td valign="top">Awwh. I gave this a ten. I thought it would''ve gone alot higher. =(<br/>Well, I like it.<br/>I really thought it was good.</td>', 'Awwh. I gave this a ten. I thought it would''ve gone alot higher. =(Well, I like it.I really thought it was good.', '2007-04-04 23:06:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(3521010, 489068, 42013, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Bear_Music:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by inshaala:</b></div><hr/><i> Nice shot and the rainbow tops it off (i sympathise with you about the dust - i think mine is probably the same) - although i wouldnt recommend a polarising filter for use with a rainbow, arent rainbows by essence polarised light? ND is the way to go :) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Not a problem, you can rotate the polarizer so it is NOT polarizing the rainbow and then it''s just a ND filter in effect.<br/> <br/>Great shot. Peters :-) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks! I used the polarizer on my Tree minimal editing shot and I think it appeared to intensify the colors because it darkened the sky. I thought a polarizer brightened the rainbow, but it is an illusion caused by relative brightness.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-04-04 23:22:56.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by Bear_Music: Originally posted by inshaala: Nice shot and the rainbow tops it off (i sympathise with you about the dust - i think mine is probably the same) - although i wouldnt recommend a polarising filter for use with a rainbow, arent rainbows by essence polarised light? ND is the way to go :)   Not a problem, you can rotate the polarizer so it is NOT polarizing the rainbow and then it''s just a ND filter in effect. Great shot. Peters :-)   Thanks! I used the polarizer on my Tree minimal editing shot and I think it appeared to intensify the colors because it darkened the sky. I thought a polarizer brightened the rainbow, but it is an illusion caused by relative brightness.', '2007-04-05 00:18:25.000000', '2007-04-05 00:22:56.000000', 0),
(3521026, 486981, 48245, '<td valign="top">pretty cool!</td>', 'pretty cool!', '2007-04-05 00:25:24.000000', NULL, 0),
